      "widgets_values": [
        "Krea 2 V10 counter-example workflow\n\nNew in V10: Guide direction. A card set to away from this image becomes a\ncounter-example - the stack isolates what that image contributes and re-adds\nit negatively, steering the result AWAY from whatever aspect the card's job\nselects.\n\nThis graph:\n- Card 1 - keep the same subject (0.80, toward this image): a normal content anchor.\n- Card 2 - suggest the visual style (0.25, away from this image): \"not this look\" - the palette, finish, and atmosphere of the style source are pushed out of the result.\n\nRules of thumb for away cards:\n- Start low: 0.10 to 0.30. Repulsion gets strange faster than attraction.\n- Give the card a specific job. away + suggest the visual style means \"not this style\"; away + suggest the color palette means \"not this palette\"; away + keep the same subject means \"not this subject\".\n- A counter-example card always avoids subject copying, whatever Subject copying says.\n- The stack report marks away cards and shows their negative targets.\n- Pair an away card with a written prompt or a toward card that says what you DO want; pure repulsion with no positive signal wanders.\n\nThe V10 feedback outputs still apply: the stack report (Preview Any) narrates\nthe away card's targets, and the prepared-references sheet (Preview Image)\nshows what the encoder studied for both cards.\n\nLearn more:\n- docs/krea-v10-user-guide.md\n- docs/nodes/kg-krea-2-image-guide-card-v10.md\n"
      ],
      "title": "How the V10 counter-example works",
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    }
